\section{The Interference Graph and Its Invariant}

In Chaitin and colleagues' \emph{Register Allocation via Coloring}, a
vertex represents a live range and an edge means the adjacent ranges
must receive distinct registers. Begin with one class of $K$
interchangeable registers, with instructions whose inputs are read
before their outputs are written. Backward liveness gives the set of
values live after each instruction. For each definition $d$, add edges
from $d$ to the other values live afterward. Treat simultaneous live-ins
as jointly live at a synthetic entry if they have no defining
instructions in the graph. At a pure copy $d\gets s$, omit the edge
that \emph{this definition} would add between $d$ and $s$: after the
copy they hold the same value. Keep an edge if some other instruction
establishes interference, and record the copy as a preference regardless.
For example, a value used for the last time by $d$'s defining instruction
can share its register with $d$ in this model; a live input to a later
instruction cannot.

Simplification removes a vertex of current degree less than $K$ and
pushes it onto a stack. When reinstated in reverse order, it has fewer
than $K$ already colored neighbors and must have a free color. If no
such vertex remains, failure is not yet proved. Pereira's \emph{A Survey
on Register Allocation} describes Briggs, Cooper, and Torczon's
optimistic coloring: remove a potential spill anyway and wait until
selection to find out if its neighbors exhaust the colors. With two
registers, a four-cycle has no initial vertex of degree less than two,
yet is colorable: remove one vertex optimistically, color the remaining
path, and observe that the two neighbors of the removed vertex get the
same color.

\section{Why Copy Coalescing Needs a Guard}

Coalescing merges the endpoints of a copy preference. The merged vertex
inherits the union of the two neighbor sets, and a successful coloring
then makes the copy redundant. Noninterference of the endpoints is
necessary but not sufficient to preserve colorability. With $K=2$,
consider the path $a-u-v-b$. It is bipartite, yet identifying $a$ with
$b$ turns it into a triangle. In fact the two endpoints of the original
path must get opposite colors, so the allocator must pay for that copy
if it wants to avoid spilling under these constraints.

\Cref{fig:visual-232} makes the two-register obstruction explicit:
merging the copy endpoints introduces a three-way conflict.

