\section{Color the Homes after Choosing the Spills}

Selecting spill candidates does not decide how many bytes the stack
frame needs. Distinct spilled values can share a slot if their memory
copies never need to coexist. As a first implementation, consider
one straight-line block with one store per spill home, no uses outside
the block, and equally sized, equally aligned homes. Build intervals
from the rewritten stores through the last reload of each home.
Use ordered memory subpositions: an interval ends just after its last
read. This is memory liveness, not the liveness of the short register
temporaries introduced by the rewrite.

\begin{sudocode}[Reuse one-size spill homes]
\State $active\gets$ min-heap ordered by end; $free\gets\emptyset$
\ForAll{$h$ in order of first store}
  \While{$active$ nonempty and its first end $\le h.start$}
    \State put the first entry's slot into $free$
    \State pop the first entry \Comment{Its last reload is past}
  \EndWhile
  \If{$free$ is empty}
    \State reserve one aligned slot and put it in $free$
  \EndIf
  \State $slot[h]\gets$ remove a slot from $free$
  \State insert $(h.end,slot[h])$ into $active$
    \Comment{Reserve through its final read}
\EndFor
\State \Return $slot$
\end{sudocode}

Take four-byte homes with intervals $p=[1,6)$, $q=[4,9)$,
and $r=[6,8)$. Store 17, 29, and 41 respectively. If $p$ gets
offset 0, $q$ gets offset 4, and $r$ reuses offset 0; the
frame payload for the homes is eight bytes. Reloads must still
observe $p=17$ before point 6, $r=41$ after its store, and $q=29$
afterward. A mutant that reuses offset 0 for $q$ would corrupt
the last reload of $p$. A value with no reload and no exit obligation
needs no home in this model; remove the redundant store before building
its interval.

At each store the occupied slots are exactly those reserved for still
active intervals. Reserving a fresh slot therefore witnesses simultaneous
demand, so the number reserved is minimal for these assumptions. Sorting
and heap operations cost $O(H\log H)$ for $H$ homes. With branches, a
single textual last reload may not exist; derive interference from
control-flow liveness instead. Variable-size slots additionally require
byte-range and alignment checks.
